<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thiksey Monastery</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thiksey Monastery is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> Buddhist monastery on a hilltop about 19 km east of Leh in Ladakh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Founded in the 15th century, its tiers of white buildings rising up the hill resemble the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. It houses a large statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> made in the 1970s, as well as many stupas, thangkas and wall paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thiksey-monastery.jpg" alt="Thiksey Monastery" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aksveer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thikse_Monastery_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The monastery stands at 3,600 metres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> and is the largest in central Ladakh. Its buildings climb the hill in ascending order of importance, from the monks' dwellings at the foot to the temples and the residence of the chief lama at the top, which has given it the name Mini Potala. The main building is twelve storeys high, painted red, ochre and white, and built as a fort monastery in the Central Tibetan pattern, like Likir and Rangdum. From it the gompa at Matho, the palace at Stok and the former royal palace at Shey can all be seen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Je Tsongkhapa</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, sent his disciple Jangsem Sherap Zangpo to the King of Ladakh with a small statue of Amitayus containing bone powder and a drop of Tsongkhapa's own blood. With the king's help Sherab Zangpo founded a small monastery, Lhakhang Serpo, the Yellow Temple, at Stagmo in 1433. His pupil Palden Zangpo then built the larger house at Thiksey; legend says two crows carried off a ritual torma offering and set it down on the site, which he took as a sign.</p><p>Thiksey became second in Ladakh only to Hemis, administering ten monasteries such as Diskit, Spituk, Likir and Stok, and held 1,327 acres of land and some 25 villages. Around 1770 younger princes of the royal house, among them Jigsmet Namgyal, were placed there as lamas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Maitreya Temple, built to mark the visit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> in 1970, holds a 15-metre statue of clay, gold paint and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> made over four years by local artists under Nawang Tsering of the Central Institute of Buddhist Studies, the largest such statue in Ladakh, shown seated in the lotus position. The assembly hall keeps 225 volumes of the Tengyur. The monastery supports the nunnery at Nyerma, on land it gave after the founding of the Ladakh Nuns Association in 1996. Its yearly Gustor festival, with masked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> dances, falls in the ninth Tibetan month.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thikse Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries such as Alchi, Hemis and <b>Thiksey</b> held great influence.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ladakh">Kingdom of Ladakh</a></li></ul></section>
